WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:05.339 --> 00:00:13.840
Paul, the spotlight once again comes around
onto you for the consolidated month seven.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.439
I was not going to spend long on this, because
the story moves on very much from what we

00:00:18.439 --> 00:00:21.070
talked about the last time we met.

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:27.380
There are three things we are trying to do
here, one of which is to secure a bottom line

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:31.250
which is in line with our commitment to have
a balance this year.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:38.200
You will see that, in the forecast, we have
got to a £31 million overspend.

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:42.140
That was significantly bigger when we last
met, so that has continued to improve over

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:43.170
time.

00:00:43.170 --> 00:00:47.630
You can see that, when we bring in the risks
and mitigations, that moves to a small positive,

00:00:47.630 --> 00:00:49.060
£135 million.

00:00:49.060 --> 00:00:54.500
So we are moving in the right direction in
order to hit our commitment to be balanced

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.469
on the bottom line.

00:00:56.469 --> 00:01:02.890
The second thing, in the context of the provider
deficit that is emerging, we need to do every

00:01:02.890 --> 00:01:08.649
possible thing that we can to improve on that
financial position without unintended consequences.

00:01:08.649 --> 00:01:13.829
Therefore, we are working very hard around
the system, just to see what potential there

00:01:13.829 --> 00:01:19.369
is to unlock benefits.

00:01:19.369 --> 00:01:23.549
That could see some improvement in the bottom-line
position that is in the report, but I am not

00:01:23.549 --> 00:01:26.530
able to confirm that at this stage.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:31.040
The third thing, and perhaps the most important,
is that, whatever the bottom line we achieve

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:34.700
this year, it will have been achieved with
substantial non-recurrent benefits, which

00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:37.679
is a theme I have referred to on a number
of occasions.

00:01:37.679 --> 00:01:41.799
It has a substantial amount of underspend
on the central programme costs, for example,

00:01:41.799 --> 00:01:46.429
which, as I mentioned in the allocations paper,
will be severely cut next year.

00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:51.109
Therefore, the opportunity to use it will
not be quite so strong next year.

00:01:51.109 --> 00:01:57.770
It has the savings in the continuing healthcare
settlement; savings only in time terms, because

00:01:57.770 --> 00:01:59.029
that will come round.

00:01:59.029 --> 00:02:02.719
We need to make a more resilient position
for next year.

00:02:02.719 --> 00:02:07.909
So the sorts of thing we are doing there,
just to keep the Board informed: the resilience

00:02:07.909 --> 00:02:13.200
work that I have referred to before in this
group is going apace, so that we work with

00:02:13.200 --> 00:02:18.460
those CCGs that have resilience problems,
if I can put it that way, to ensure that they

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:21.180
are sorted out at source.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.670
As I have mentioned already, the CDF – the
Cancer Drugs Fund – is the biggest single

00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:29.569
adverse variance that we have this year in
our position.

00:02:29.569 --> 00:02:33.770
Through the consultation, we expect to get
that to a position that can be managed next

00:02:33.770 --> 00:02:34.770
year.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:37.950
The third thing, which is clearly not so much
an issue for next year but something that

00:02:37.950 --> 00:02:43.931
needs to be online for next year to secure
a better financial position, is the Right

00:02:43.931 --> 00:02:48.920
Care Programme, which briefly got mentioned
in the allocations paper but is gearing itself

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:53.250
up for the first wave of CCGs in the next
few months.

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:58.340
That will be another major positive that will
come on stream as we go into next year.

00:02:58.340 --> 00:03:01.810
So, numbers going in the right direction in
a small way at the moment.

00:03:01.810 --> 00:03:08.030
I think we can take some assurance that we
will hit our balanced bottom line.

00:03:08.030 --> 00:03:15.099
We are doing our best to contribute to the
overall financial position of the NHS.

00:03:15.099 --> 00:03:18.180
Does the chair of audit wish to add anything?

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:19.180
I do not think so.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:20.640
I do not think there is anything to add, Chair.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.640
Thank you.

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:22.640
Anybody else?

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:23.640
No.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:24.640
Thank you, Paul.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:26.110
Let us take the report and the assurance that
you give us.

